od Johnson the first  James Wood John Cena and Edward Mead  Johnson while Jan Jade did start off as  a family business it never really went  through that stage of being a  bootstrapped garage startup you see it  Robert was already a pretty successful  businessman having founded a company  called the seabarian Johnson which  produced sterile Searcher equipment by  1878 this company alone was producing at  ten thousand dollars per month in  profits that itself is a solid number  but when you account for inflation it  becomes 296 000 per month so yeah Robert  was killing it unfortunately though the  founders would end up having a  disagreement and Robert would eventually  leave the business but not before  becoming extremely wealthy combine this  with the fact fact that his brothers  were also well off and talented in their  own right and starting up Johnson  Johnson was a breeze the trio had a  capital knowledge experience and  connections from day one so they hit the  ground running they hired 14 employees  and launched a line of ready to use  surgical supplies and that marked the  beginning of jnj in 1886. over the next  few years the brothers aggressively grew  their product line and made history  within the surgical industry they  produced the world's first aerosurgical  products including sterile sutures  cotton and the gods and in 1894 they  launched their signature baby powder by  this point J had only been in business  for a mere eight years but they were  already employing 400 people and running  14 in buildings not to mention it they  were also becoming a household name but  just because they were successful and  Rich did not mean that they were evil or  unethical in fact there is very little  evidence suggesting that any of the  brothers had ill intentions or moral  motivations there's actually a bunch of  evidence suggesting the exact opposite  during the Spanish-American war for  example jnj donated 300 000 surgical  dressings and created trauma stretchers  for the soldiers they also made  substantial donations to the relief  efforts of the 1990 Galveston hurricane  and the 1906 San Francisco earthquake  and these weren't just for publicity  either and you could tell based on how  they treated their employees for example  when the 1901 smallpox epidemic hit J  would sponsor the vaccination of all  their employees similarly during the  Great Depression J J did not even lay  off a single employee they actually  raised wages by five percent jnj was  also ahead of their time when it came to  incorporating women into the workforce  eight out of their first 14 employees  were women and over the next few decades  women accounted for roughly half of  their company's Workforce and led a  quarter of their departments but then  how did such an Innovative  forward-thinking and a generous company  devolve into what they are today well it  all started with the brothers dying off  one by one Robert was the first one to  go in 1910 dying of chronic kidney  disease 22 years later James would die  while returning from a vacation on the  RMS Majestic and finally in 1934 Edward  would pass away from a heart attack  within just 10 years of the last founder  dying J J would be taken public in 1944  and that marked the beginning of their  moral free fall  Johnson and Johnson didn't just become  an immoral overnight though after all  the company was led by the brothers  children who carried many of the same  values and the principles as the  brothers themselves in fact when the  company was taken public Robert Sana  Robert Wood Johnson II wrote an ethical  guidelines book called orquito this  document outlined how choices had been  made at the company for the past 60  years and how they should continue being  made moving forward essentially the  Creator advises leaders at the company  to put the needs and the well-being of  the people they serve over profits and  it looks like this is what they were  doing at the beginning a perfect exampl